\begin{lemma}[Jordan-neighborhood basis]\label{lem:jordan-basis}
Let \(K\Subset U\subset\mathbb C\) be a nonempty full continuum and let \(\mathcal O\) be any neighborhood of \(K\) compactly contained in \(U\).  Then there is a Jordan domain \(V\) with
\[
 K\subset V\subset\overline V\subset\mathcal O.               \tag{3.1}
\]
Moreover there is a nested sequence of such Jordan domains with
\[
 K\subset V_{r+1},\qquad \overline V_{r+1}\subset V_r,\qquad \bigcap_{r\ge1}\overline V_r=K.  \tag{3.2}
\]
\end{lemma}

theorems.proof_latex:
\begin{proof}
If \(K\) is a singleton, round disks centered at that point satisfy (3.1) and (3.2).  Otherwise, fullness and connectedness of \(K\) make \(\widehat{\mathbb C}\setminus K\) a simply connected domain of the sphere containing \(\infty\).  Uniformize it by the exterior of the closed unit disk, normalized at infinity: let \(\Phi\colon\{\lvert w\rvert>1\}\cup\{\infty\}\to\widehat{\mathbb C}\setminus K\) be the conformal isomorphism with \(\Phi(\infty)=\infty\).  For each radius \(r>1\) the image \(\Phi(\{\lvert w\rvert=r\})\) is a Jordan curve in the complement of \(K\), and its bounded complementary side \(V(r)\) contains \(K\).  Every point \(z\) of the complement of \(K\) has uniformizing modulus \(\lvert\Phi^{-1}(z)\rvert\) strictly greater than \(1\), so \(z\notin\overline{V(r)}\) as soon as \(r<\lvert\Phi^{-1}(z)\rvert\); hence \(\bigcap_{r>1}\overline{V(r)}=K\).  The closed sets \(\overline{V(r)}\setminus\mathcal O\) are compact, decrease as \(r\downarrow1\), and have empty intersection, so some \(V=V(r)\) with \(r\) sufficiently close to \(1\) satisfies \(\overline V\subset\mathcal O\); this gives (3.1).  Choosing the radii \(r_k\downarrow1\) strictly monotonically gives the nested sequence of (3.2), and the intersection equals \(K\) because every point of the complement has uniformizing modulus strictly greater than \(1\).
\end{proof}

theorems.statement_latex:
\begin{lemma}[One quadratic pullback]\label{lem:quadratic-pullback}
Let \(q\colon U'\to U\) be a proper holomorphic map of degree two between plane topological disks, and let \(K\Subset U\) be a nonempty full continuum.  Let \(c\) be the unique critical point of \(q\) and \(v=q(c)\).
\begin{enumerate}
\item If \(v\in K\), then \(q^{-1}K\) is a single full continuum, and the local degree of \(q\) on it is two.
\item If \(v\notin K\), then \(q^{-1}K\) is the disjoint union of two full continua, each mapped homeomorphically onto \(K\) by \(q\).
\end{enumerate}
\end{lemma}

\begin{corollary}[Finite full \(p\)-fold pullback]\label{cor:finite-pullback}
Under a chain of \(p\) quadratic-like pullbacks, the inverse image of one full continuum is a union of at most \(2^p\) pairwise disjoint full continua.  Every resulting component maps onto its parent continuum with positive integer degree.  The inverse image of a finite pairwise-disjoint family of full continua is again a finite pairwise-disjoint family of full continua.
\end{corollary}

theorems.proof_latex:
\begin{proof}
Apply Lemma \ref{lem:quadratic-pullback} successively along the chain.  At each stage every parent continuum has either one degree-two child or two degree-one children, and every child maps onto its parent.  Children of different parents are disjoint because their images lie in the disjoint parent continua.  Induction over the \(p\) stages gives the bound \(2^p\), the stated surjectivity, and a positive product degree for every terminal component.  Applying this separately to the members of a finite pairwise-disjoint family proves the last statement.
\end{proof}

theorems.statement_latex:
\begin{corollary}[Isolating Jordan disks]\label{cor:isolating-disks}
Let \(C_1,\dots,C_N\Subset U\) be pairwise disjoint full continua.  For each \(i\) there is a Jordan domain \(D_i\Subset U\) such that
\[
 C_i\subset D_i,\qquad \overline D_i\cap C_j=\varnothing\quad(j\ne i).  \tag{3.3}
\]
The \(D_i\) may be chosen pairwise disjoint.
\end{corollary}

theorems.proof_latex:
\begin{proof}
A finite pairwise-disjoint family of compacta in \(U\) has positive minimum pairwise distance and positive distance from \(\partial U\).  Apply Lemma \ref{lem:jordan-basis} inside pairwise-disjoint metric neighborhoods of the \(C_i\).
\end{proof}

