\begin{proposition}[Excess central pullback]\label{prop:excess}
There is a component
\[
 X\subset F^{-1}A,\qquad X\ne A.                    \tag{4.1}
\]
Every such component satisfies
\[
 X\cap B=\varnothing.                                \tag{4.2}
\]
\end{proposition}

\begin{remark}[Complete component certificate]\label{rem:certificate}
By Corollary \ref{cor:finite-pullback} the family of excess components is finite.  Fix any deterministic order on the finite pulled-component inventory and choose the first \(X\); no theorem depends on this choice.  Its complete finite itinerary is
\[
 \tau(X)=\bigl(X,\ f(X),\dots,f^p(X)=A;\ \varsigma_0,\dots,\varsigma_{p-1}\bigr),  \tag{4.4}
\]
where \(\varsigma_r\) records the actual component (sheet) of \(f^{-1}(f^{r+1}X)\) containing \(f^rX\).  If a stage already carries external or puzzle markings, those markings order the \(\varsigma_r\); otherwise (4.4) is a geometric component itinerary, not an invented symbolic word.  By Corollaries \ref{cor:finite-pullback} and \ref{cor:isolating-disks} there is a Jordan domain \(D_X\Subset U^p\) with
\[
 X\subset D_X,\qquad \overline D_X\cap\bigl((F^{-1}B\setminus X)\cup B\bigr)=\varnothing.  \tag{4.5}
\]
The peripheral access used in the collision is the based homotopy class of \(\partial D_X\): a genuine access in the complement of the actual pulled obstacle family, not a ribbon-tree label.
\end{remark}

\begin{theorem}[Forced collision at ordinary primitive canonical seeds]\label{thm:forced-collision}
Let \(f\colon U^1\to U^0\) be an ordinary quadratic-like restriction of a quadratic polynomial, primitively renormalizable of period \(p>1\), and let
\[
 \widehat i\colon T'_A\longrightarrow T_A
\]
be Kahn's localized inclusion of the canonical renormalization (the dynamical localization of \((i^p,F)\) at \(A=K_p\) with removal of \(B=\mathcal K_p\)).  Then \(\widehat i\) is \emph{not injective}.  More precisely, there are a component \(X\ne A\) of \(F^{-1}A\), a based loop \(\gamma\subset U^p\setminus F^{-1}B\), and localization subgroups \(H_s\), \(H_t\) such that
\[
 [\gamma]\notin H_s,\qquad (i^p)_*[\gamma]=1\in H_t,   \tag{1.2}
\]
and the lift of \(\gamma\) to the source localization cover has distinct endpoints which \(\widehat i\) identifies.
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}[Collision certificate]\label{rem:collision-certificate}
The proof yields the explicit certificate
\[
 \mathfrak C_{\mathrm{can}}=\bigl(X,\ D_X,\ [\gamma],\ \tau(X),\ H_s,\ H_t,\ H_s,\ H_s[\gamma],\ \widehat z_*,\ \widehat z_*',\ \widehat a(\widehat z_*)\bigr),  \tag{6.3}
\]
recording the first excess component, the peripheral access, the complete \(p\)-step component itinerary, the source and target subgroups, the two distinguished source cosets, the distinct source sheets, and the common target point.  No critical-value crossing and no numerical approximation is used to create the collision.
\end{remark}

theorems.proof_latex:
\begin{proof}
Take \(X\), \(D_X\), \(\gamma\), \(H_s\), \(H_t\) from Proposition \ref{prop:excess}, Remark \ref{rem:certificate}, and Theorem \ref{thm:subgroup-defect}; then (1.2) is (5.5)--(5.6).

Let \(\pi_s\colon\widehat\Omega_s\to\Omega_s\) and \(\pi_t\colon\widehat\Omega_t\to\Omega_t\) be the localization covers, corresponding to the subgroups \(H_s\) and \(H_t\) (Kahn, \texttt{psiql2.tex}, ll.~434--505), and let \(\widehat a\colon\widehat\Omega_s\to\widehat\Omega_t\) be the unique commuting lift of \(a=i^p\).  Choose a source lift \(\widehat z_*\in\pi_s^{-1}(z_*)\) and lift \(\gamma\) from \(\widehat z_*\); write \(\widehat z_*'\) for the terminal endpoint of the lifted path.  By the covering-subgroup endpoint criterion --- a loop lifts closed at \(\widehat z_*\) exactly when its class lies in \(H_s\) --- together with (5.5),
\[
 \widehat z_*'\ne\widehat z_*.                        \tag{6.1}
\]
The \(\widehat a\)-image of the lifted path is the target lift, from \(\widehat a(\widehat z_*)\), of the loop \(a\circ\gamma\).  By (5.6) the class of \(a\circ\gamma\) is trivial, in particular lies in \(H_t\), so this target lift is closed.  Therefore
\[
 \widehat a(\widehat z_*')=\widehat a(\widehat z_*).  \tag{6.2}
\]
Both points lie in the complementary annulus part of the localized source.  Completing the localized marked surfaces by adjoining the marked continuum \(A\) glues \(A\) in along a local neighborhood and does not identify distinct points of the complement cover.  Thus (6.1)--(6.2) exhibit two distinct points of \(T'_A\) with equal \(\widehat i\)-image in \(T_A\): an actual point collision of Kahn's localized inclusion.

In path language: if \(\alpha\) is any source path from the marked base access to \(z_*\), the two classes \([\alpha]\) and \([\alpha\cdot\gamma]\) are distinct in the source localization and equal after target localization.  Kahn's forward path-equivalence statement (\texttt{psiql2.tex}, ll.~612--619) --- source-equivalent paths become target-equivalent --- is respected; it is the (unstated) converse that fails.
\end{proof}

